<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Theme:</b> I Expect My Future to Be...</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="margin: 0 0 4px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze the structure of various texts. Reading: Interpret characters' motives in short stories. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
      <p style="margin: 4px 0 0 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze the structure of texts related to future education planning (e.g., "Find the introduction and conclusion in this study guide"). Can interpret characters' motives in stories about planning for the future (e.g., "Why does the student want to study science"). Can identify and produce the minimal pair sounds to enhance their pronunciation and sound discrimination skills (e.g., /θ/ in "math" and /s/ in "mass").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">Stage & Description</p></th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">Activities & Teacher Actions</p></th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">Estimated Date and Time</p></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows students four images on the board or projector: a university campus, a hospital, a business office, and a research lab. Students brainstorm (in pairs) what kind of "future", "career", or "goals" they associate with each image, using vocabulary from Lesson 1 (e.g., "This could be a 'business' 'opportunity'"). The teacher elicits a few ideas from the class, asking "What 'plans' do people make for these places?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a short reading passage (e.g., a simple paragraph about a student's plans for university). The teacher reads the title and first sentence aloud, then models thinking aloud about what the text might be about: "The title is 'My Ambitious Goals.' I see the word 'university'. So, I expect this text will be about someone's 'future' 'plans' for education." The teacher emphasizes how to use context and prior vocabulary to predict content.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we read every single word quickly at first, or do we look for the main idea?" (Main idea)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If the title is 'Future Career in Business', will the text be about a doctor?" (No)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative text (approx. 100-120 words) titled "Maria's Future Vision" about a student, Maria, who has specific "goals" and "plans" for her "career" after high school, mentioning her desire to "achieve" them, "development" in "business", and stating she "has to" study hard. The text includes examples of future continuous ("In five years, I will be working as an engineer") and WH-questions ("What will I do to prepare?").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret basic written information about personal aspirations and future plans.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text quickly to answer: "What is Maria's main 'goal' for her 'future'?" (Possible answer: To become an engineer and work in business.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read the text again more carefully and mark the following statements as True or False:
              <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Maria wants to study medicine. (False)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">She "has to" "prepare" for university. (True)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In five years, she "will be working as an engineer." (True)</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match specific quotes from the text to Maria’s feelings or actions. E.g., "Why does Maria say 'I must work hard'?" Match to: "She wants to 'achieve' her 'ambitious' 'goals'."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), WH-questions, future continuous. Vocabulary: future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, ambitious, important, future-oriented, achieve, prepare, set. Phonemic Awareness: Minimal pairs /θ/ in "math" and /s/ in "mass." Pragmatic: Discussing goals.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Cloze Activity:</b> Students receive a printed version of "Maria's Future Vision" with 5-7 key words (e.g., 'future', 'goals', 'must', 'will be working', 'ambitious', 'prepare') removed. Students reread the text and fill in the blanks with the correct words from a provided word bank. The teacher checks answers as a class, focusing on correct word choice and context.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar and Vocabulary Spotting:</b> Students work in pairs to re-read the text and highlight all sentences that use "have to" or "must," future continuous ("will be working"), and any WH-questions related to "future" "plans" or "goals." They write one example for each grammar point in their notebooks. E.g., "You 'have to' send the application before the deadline."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation Focus - Minimal Pairs (Reading Aloud):</b> The teacher introduces the minimal pairs /θ/ (e.g., 'math', 'think') and /s/ (e.g., 'mass', 'sink'). The teacher models the sounds. Students then read a short list of words (e.g., "math/mass", "thin/sin", "thought/sought", "path/pass") aloud in pairs, focusing on clear pronunciation. They underline these words if they appear in the main reading text, or practice a short paragraph containing similar sounds if not.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">15 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Reading Analysis and Presentation</b>
          Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives a different short text (or a different paragraph from a longer text) related to "Future Plans and Goals." These texts could describe other students' "ambitious" "career" "goals," different paths to "achieve" them, or "opportunities" for "development" in various fields.
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Read and Analyze:</b> Each group reads their assigned text carefully to "analyze the structure" (e.g., identify the introduction, main points about "plans," and conclusion) and "interpret characters' motives" (e.g., "Why does this character want to become an entrepreneur?"). They also identify at least two examples of modals ("have to," "must") and one future continuous structure from their text.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Summary:</b> In their groups, students collaboratively create a short summary (3-4 sentences) of their text's main character's "future" "vision" and "goals," using at least three of the target vocabulary words (e.g., 'ambitious', 'opportunity', 'development'). They also include a sentence about what the character "has to" do to "achieve" their "plan."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Share Out:</b> Each group presents their summary to the class. The teacher encourages classmates to ask WH-questions (e.g., "What are their 'plans'?", "Why "must" they study 'carefully'?") about the presented summaries. This promotes sociolinguistic competence by engaging in collaborative goal-setting discussions, offering suggestions and feedback on the characters' 'plans'.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">12-15 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and summary presentation.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete an individual "Reading Response Card" where they interpret a character's motive. They read a short paragraph (2-3 sentences) describing a student's action (e.g., "Juan stays late after school to study 'math' every day.") and then answer: "Why "must" Juan study 'carefully' every day? What 'goal' is he trying to 'achieve'?" Students are also asked to identify if any words contain the /θ/ or /s/ sounds they practiced.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies main motive from the text (Interpretation).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., 'goal', 'achieve', 'must') correctly in response.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Optional) Evidences correct pronunciation of /θ/ and /s/ during group presentation/sharing.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates effectively in group analysis.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one 'important' thing you learned about interpreting a character's 'future' 'plans' today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I now identify the main 'goals' in a short text?" (Yes/No/A Little)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">8 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about 'future' 'goals' was easiest for you today? What was most challenging?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can understanding other people's 'plans' help you with your own 'vision'?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The group reading analysis activity worked well for promoting collaborative interpretation, and students were able to use the target vocabulary when summarizing. Some students still struggle to identify the "structure" of the text (introduction/conclusion). The minimal pair pronunciation practice was engaging but needs more reinforcement. For the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), I need to scaffold speaking prompts more explicitly to ensure all students confidently discuss their own "future" "plans" and "goals," building directly on the vocabulary and comprehension from today's reading texts.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson will transition from reading about others' future plans to students actively discussing and interacting about their own "future" "goals" and "career" "opportunities," directly using the vocabulary and grammar structures practiced today.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>